{"id":"h156","run":2,"role":"test_right","category":"science-chemistry","question":"In the infrared spectrum of $\\mathrm{H}^{127} \\mathrm{I}$, there is an intense line at $2309 \\mathrm{~cm}^{-1}$. Calculate the force constant of $\\mathrm{H}^{127} \\mathrm{I}$.\nIs the correct answer \"325 $\\mathrm{~N} \\cdot \\mathrm{m}^{-1}$\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct force constant of H127I is about 314 N/m, not 325 N/m.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The force constant follows from k = μω² with ω = 2πc·ν̃.","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The reduced mass of HI is about 0.99988 u, or 1.6603e-27 kg.","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The angular frequency is about 4.349e14 rad/s, so ω² is about 1.891e29 s⁻².","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Multiplying μ by ω² gives k of about 314 N/m.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"325 N/m is the same order of magnitude and small variations in constants shift the result slightly, which might support it.","confidence":0.4,"lean":1,"theta":0.6283,"phi":-0.0},{"text":"No plausible variation in constants or masses raises the result to 325 N/m.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The roughly 3.5% difference is too large to be explained by rounding.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Using simplified masses (H = 1, I = 127) gives about 312 N/m, which is also not 325.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":-0.8,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":8,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":4,"J":0.7